Here's a side-by-side of the three on the axes that usually decide the choice.

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Stack([cmp, tbl])
cmp = Comparison("React vs Vue vs Svelte", [react, vue, svelte])
react = {name:"React",attributes:["Meta, 2013 · MIT","JSX, functions + hooks","Virtual DOM, re-renders at runtime","~42 kB (react + react-dom, min+gzip)","Next.js, React Native, Remix","Largest talent pool and ecosystem","You assemble the stack yourself"]}
vue = {name:"Vue",attributes:["Evan You, 2014 · MIT","SFCs: template + script + style","Fine-grained reactivity, optional VDOM","~34 kB runtime (Vue 3, min+gzip)","Nuxt, Vuetify, Quasar","Batteries-included official router/store","Gentlest learning curve of the three"]}
svelte = {name:"Svelte",attributes:["Rich Harris, 2016 · MIT","SFCs, compiles away the framework","No VDOM — compiled reactive updates","Smallest output (~5-15 kB for a small app)","SvelteKit","Lean and fast, smaller ecosystem","Fewer jobs, but strong DX advocates"]}
tbl = Table("At a glance", ["Dimension","React","Vue","Svelte"], [["First release","2013","2014","2016"],["Weekly npm downloads","~25M","~6M","~1.5M"],["GitHub stars","~230k","~208k","~81k"],["Rendering","Virtual DOM","Virtual DOM + reactivity","Compiled, no VDOM"],["Language","JSX","Template (or JSX)","Template (or JSX)"],["TypeScript","Very good","Very good","Very good, built-in"],["Best fit","Huge apps, hiring","Teams wanting defaults","Perf- and size-sensitive apps"]])
:::

**How to pick:** React if hiring, ecosystem breadth, or cross-platform (React Native) matter most. Vue if you want an official router/store/SSR story without assembling one and a template syntax that onboards juniors fast. Svelte if bundle size and runtime speed dominate — a marketing site, embedded widget, or edge-rendered app where every kilobyte counts.

The one thing all three share: component-based architecture with scoped styles, so the mental model transfers between them. The numbers above are approximate and move month to month — check npm trends before committing on popularity alone.

AWS er den brede, modne markedslederen; Azure er sterkest der Microsoft-stacken og hybrid drift allerede står sentralt.

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Comparison("AWS vs. Azure", [aws, azure])
aws = {name:"AWS", attributes:["Markedsleder (~30 % andel)","Størst tjenestebredde (200+ tjenester)","Sterkest på IaaS og serverless","Moden dokumentasjon og økosystem","Prising: detaljert, lett å overforbruke","35+ regioner, høyest modenhet"]}
azure = {name:"Azure", attributes:["Nr. 2 (~22 % andel)","Bredest geografisk dekning (60+ regioner)","Sterkest på hybrid (Azure Arc) og Microsoft-stack","Best om du bruker Entra ID, Windows Server, M365","Enterprise-avtaler og lisensfordeler","Prising: rabatter via EA og hybrid-fordeler"]}
:::

Kort sagt: velg AWS når du vil ha maksimal tjenestebredde, mest modne verktøy og frihet fra leverandørbindinger i applikasjonslaget. Velg Azure når du allerede har Microsoft-avtaler, kjører Windows/AD-tunge arbeidslaster eller trenger hybrid sky på en enkel måte. Mange større virksomheter ender med begge — ofte AWS for nye sky-native tjenester og Azure for det som henger sammen med Microsoft-plattformen.

